      "source": [
        "In this example, PCR and especially PLS tend to outperform ridge and lasso.\n",
        "\n",
        "- PCR can recover the signal if enough components are included, even though the signal lies in low-variance directions.\n",
        "- PLS performs particularly well because it uses $y$ when constructing components, allowing it to directly identify directions associated with the response.\n",
        "- Ridge and lasso shrink coefficients based on the observed predictors. Since the signal is weak (low variance) and spread across many features through $A_1$, it is harder for them to isolate and recover it.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-caution}\n",
        "Important predictive directions do not have to correspond to directions of large variance.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- \n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Why does OLS still perform well?\n",
        "\n",
        "In this example, OLS achieves strong performance, even though $X$ is high-dimensional and largely driven by noise. This may seem surprising at first, but it can be explained by the structure of the data.\n",
        "\n",
        "- The response $y$ is driven by a low-dimensional latent factor $Z_1$.\n",
        "- This factor is embedded in $X$ through the term $Z_1 A_1$, so the signal lies in the column space of $X$.\n",
        "- As a result, there exists a linear combination of the predictors that can approximate $y$ well.\n",
        "\n",
        "Even when $p > n$ or $X$ is nearly singular, OLS (as implemented in `LinearRegression`) computes a minimum-norm least squares solution using the pseudo-inverse. This solution effectively restricts the fitted model to a low-complexity subspace, which can generalize well when the true signal is low-dimensional.\n",
        "\n",
        "In contrast:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Ridge and lasso operate in the original coordinate system and are influenced by the high-variance noise directions in $X$.\n",
        "- PCR relies on variance in $X$ and may require many components before reaching the predictive direction.\n",
        "- PLS performs better than PCR because it uses $y$ to guide the construction of components, allowing it to identify predictive directions more efficiently.\n",
        "\n",
        "This example illustrates that OLS does not necessarily fail in high-dimensional settings. When the signal lies in a well-represented low-dimensional subspace and the noise level is moderate, the minimum-norm solution can still achieve good predictive performance.\n",
        "::: -->"
